---
import InstrumentBand from "../../../components/lab/instrument/InstrumentBand.astro";
import PlasmaLight026 from "../../../components/studies/PlasmaLight026.svelte";
import PlasmaLight027 from "../../../components/studies/PlasmaLight027.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "plasma-light-2026-11-11");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2 id="problem">01 / A dark charge, then a borrowed flame</h2>
    <p>
      A plasma engine, a preview part outside scored play, does not answer at
      once. Holding thrust starts a charge of 0.6 seconds that already draws
      energy and moves nothing; then the engine delivers its thrust and sustains
      it until release. That delay is the engine’s character: power gathered
      before it is let go. In numbers the release is one and a half times a
      Light ion engine’s thrust and below a Heavy ion’s, so the sense of immense
      power has to come from how the engine looks, not from its force alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      In flight the engine’s light shows none of this. During the charge it
      paints no light, so on the ship a charging engine and a dead one look the
      same; only a line of text in the deck’s gutter reports the charge. When
      thrust arrives it uses the amber beam that every engine without a design
      of its own shares: the Light ion beam’s shape and length in amber, with no
      build-up of its own. Plasma has to read as the opposite of ion’s steady
      light on the same ship, next to ion stabilizers that answer at once: first
      a build-up the player can see, then a release that looks immense.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tension is between power and readability. The charge must read as
      charging, without looking like thrust. The release must feel immense on a
      Wasp or Scout, whose whole hull is shorter than a single ion beam, without
      burying the ship, the ion light beside it or the trail that shows where it
      has been.
    </p>

    <h3>Held fixed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Plasma’s flight behaviour: the 0.6 second charge, sustained thrust, and
        the reset on release or missing power.
      </li>
      <li>The engine body, the ship drawings and the ion plumes.</li>
      <li>
        The three canvas layers and their rules: World, persistent FX light with
        its fade and bloom, and fresh LFX light redrawn each frame. Engine
        plumes and other effects live only on FX or LFX; World holds what is
        solid.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Left open</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>What the charge looks like, and where on the ship it shows.</li>
      <li>
        The moment of release, and whether it differs from sustained thrust.
      </li>
      <li>
        The sustained plume: shape, colour, size and what it leaves behind.
      </li>
      <li>Which layer carries each of these.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Outside this study</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Fission’s pulsed light, which borrows the same amber beam and needs its
        own answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        Whether plasma’s delay is worth flying, and whether a player can time a
        burn by the charge; those are balance and play, not light.
      </li>
      <li>
        How well the
        <a href="/lab/flight-scene">Flight scene instrument</a>
        serves a study. This is its first use on a design question of its own,
        so part 02 shows its examples there and notes what the instrument could
        not show. It shows one ship at a time and has no readout of engine
        state. It drew only what the game draws; for this study it gained a way
        for a study to paint its own light for an engine in place of the game’s.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 11 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        Two independent reviewers with no part in the framing tested this
        section, the second without seeing the first result. The first tested it
        as first written; the section was corrected and the second tested the
        corrected text. The one claim the second run failed, the ship’s length
        in pixels, was then replaced with a comparison that run had verified.
        The section has not been tested a third time.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Metric</th>
            <th scope="col">First</th>
            <th scope="col">Corrected</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Fail</td>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              One object, plasma’s light in flight; charge, release, plume and
              layer are facets of it. The first text put the Flight scene
              instrument on trial under its own heading; it is now one of three
              named adjacent questions.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Fail</td>
            <td>Fail</td>
            <td>
              Every fixed item exists and every open item can be shown and
              judged by looking. The first text said nothing of the charge is
              visible, called plasma far stronger than ion and left out that the
              instrument draws only what the game draws. The corrected text
              failed on one claim: a Wasp or Scout is not under 30 pixels long
              in flight.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              A charging plasma engine shows a build-up on the ship, and its
              thrust has a light of its own instead of the shared amber beam.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <details id="held-thrown">
    <summary>02.1 / A press must be answered at once</summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        <strong>Retained as a finding, 11 November 2026.</strong>
        The first example gave each phase its own layer: a bead of live light at
        the nozzle that tightened as the charge filled, a wide flare thrown into
        the persistent light at release, then a short flame. Its look for plasma
        was not right and is not carried forward; the scene is removed.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it showed is that how the engine looks is part of how it plays.
        With the game’s light, the first 0.6 seconds of a press show nothing at
        the ship, and the press feels unanswered. With any light at the nozzle
        from the first frame, the same delay reads as an engine doing something.
        The finding: a press gets an immediate visible answer at the ship, even
        when what it starts arrives later. It applies to any part that charges,
        an engine or a weapon, and it is the existing principle that every
        control answers, applied to delayed parts.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A light that grows through the charge also promises more power as it
          loads. Whether plasma should make that promise is open.
        </li>
        <li>Whether the answer belongs on FX or LFX is open.</li>
        <li>
          A plume’s size follows delivered thrust, which falls as the ship nears
          its cruise ceiling. Whether plasma’s light should shrink that way is
          open.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details id="press-answer">
    <summary>02.2 / Plasma’s live light needs its residue</summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        <strong>Retained as a finding, 11 November 2026.</strong>
        Three answers to the press were compared in one scene, each changing
        only the charge: a spark of live light on the press, a small pilot flame
        in the persistent light, and a steady live glow at the nozzle. None is
        carried forward as plasma’s answer; the scene is removed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The comparison showed how the two light layers work together for plasma.
        Live light on LFX stays exactly on the ship, which is what an instant
        answer needs. On its own it leaves nothing in the persistent light,
        where the trail of the flight is held. The finding: what plasma draws
        live on LFX, a spark, a glow or the body of its plume, also leaves a
        residue in FX.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          This is a finding about plasma. Effects live only on FX or LFX, and
          beyond that no rule assigns engines to layers: either of the two stays
          open to any engine, and each engine’s own design decides. An engine
          that touched no persistent light at all is conceivable.
        </li>
        <li>
          The Flight scene instrument served to learn this and is not kept as
          the record. The written finding is the record.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details id="light-language">
    <summary>02.3 / The light language the answer has to speak</summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        A comparison of three ways to split one plume between live light and
        residue was removed. Its plain placeholder shapes did not belong to the
        game, so it could not help answer anything. Before another example, the
        study states the language plasma’s light has to be drawn in.
      </p>
      <h3>Established</h3>
      <p>
        From the <a href="/lab/studies/engine-effects">engine effects</a> and
        <a href="/lab/studies/ion-engines-2026-10-31">ion engine</a>
        studies and the effects specification:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Hi-Fi Lo-Fi.</strong>
          Sharp, sparkly, electric 1990s effects. Inexpensive rendering supplies
          the character: hard strokes, small sprites, palette changes, arcs,
          saturation, stepping, jaggedness and interference are wanted, not
          smoothed away.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ion’s plume lives on.</strong>
          An ion engine stamps one complete luminous mark, with its core, halo
          and transparent end, into the persistent light. What trails behind is
          that mark fading. An attached shape with a separate faint residue was
          rejected for ion. This is ion’s accepted identity, not a rule for
          other engines.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Live light has one precedent.</strong>
          The ion cannon and point defence draw their ray live on LFX for 80
          milliseconds and put a dispersed treatment of it into FX.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Effects follow what the game delivers.</strong>
          An effect’s activity follows delivered output and gameplay events, not
          the state of a button.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Open, for plasma</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Where the body of plasma’s light lives: stamped into the persistent
          surface, or drawn live on the ship with its residue behind it.
        </li>
        <li>
          The finding in 02.1 answers a press before any thrust is delivered.
          Whether plasma’s charge counts as something the game delivers, or the
          delivered-output rule needs an exception for it, is not settled.
        </li>
        <li>What plasma’s own mark is, distinct from ion’s.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details id="mark-two-ways">
    <summary>02.4 / Plasma’s body of light is stamped</summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        <strong>Settled, 11 November 2026.</strong>
        One proposed mark, a chain of hard shock diamonds, was drawn two ways on
        the same flight: stamped into the persistent FX surface each tick, or
        drawn live on LFX with a fainter copy stamped behind it. The body of
        plasma’s light belongs stamped in the persistent surface, where it
        blooms, adds up and becomes its own trail. Live light is kept for the
        charge at the nozzle. The scene is removed.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          This settles the layer for plasma’s thrust. It does not accept the
          diamond chain as plasma’s mark; that is the next question.
        </li>
        <li>
          Both ways showed the charge, which assumes a charge is something the
          game delivers. That is still not settled.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details id="mark">
    <summary>02.5 / What is plasma’s mark? A churning fireball</summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        With the layer settled, the open part is the mark itself: what the
        engine stamps while it thrusts. This is the question the study set out
        to answer. Three geometric marks, a chain of diamonds, thrown rings and
        a rake of parallel strokes, were removed as far from the game. They
        showed the direction: plasma’s mark is not a shape with edges but
        something closer to an explosion held at the engine, a ball of
        turbulent, violent flame.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Kept as a reference, 11 November 2026.</strong>
        The example built that ball as a sprite 16 cells across, the way 1990s
        demos built plasma: a few sine waves interfering inside a round falloff,
        with a ragged edge, cut into a stepped palette from dark red through
        amber to white. Eight frames are cached. Each tick one frame is stamped
        into the persistent surface behind the nozzle, turned a quarter at a
        time, and three hard sparks are flung from it. Through the charge a
        small copy of the ball gathers live at the nozzle.
      </p>
      <h3>What it established</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The model works.</strong>
          A cached image stamped into the FX layer each tick is the right way to
          make this light. The stamp fading into the persistent surface reads as
          flame, in the 1990s language the game wants.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>This stamp is from another genre.</strong>
          Its chunky cells are pixel art, and the game’s drawing is clean and
          closer to vector work. The mark does not have to be vectors, but it
          cannot look like a sprite from a different kind of game.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The pixel ball fails as live light.</strong>
          Drawn on LFX for the charge it looks silly. The smooth bead of 02.1,
          with gradients and fades, served better as the answer to the press.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>The stamps</h3>
      <figure class="plates stamps">
        <div>
          {[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8].map((frame) => (
            <img
              src={`/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/fireball-stamp-${frame}.png`}
              alt={`Fireball stamp, frame ${frame} of 8`}
              width="16"
              height="16"
            />
          ))}
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The eight cached frames exactly as stamped, each 16 by 16 pixels,
          shown enlarged six times. Exported from the example’s own code on 11
          November 2026.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h3>What they made in flight</h3>
      <figure class="plates results">
        <div>
          <img
            src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/fireball-charge.png"
            alt="A Wasp with a small red crackling ball at its engine nozzle"
            width="300"
            height="300"
          >
          <img
            src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/fireball-release.png"
            alt="A Wasp with a large ball of blocky amber and white fire behind its engine"
            width="300"
            height="300"
          >
          <img
            src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/fireball-thrust.png"
            alt="A Wasp trailing a soft amber smear in steady thrust"
            width="300"
            height="300"
          >
          <img
            src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/fireball-restart.png"
            alt="A Wasp at speed with a ball of fire and a long fading tail"
            width="300"
            height="300"
          >
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          A Wasp with a Plasma engine on burn B1 at four times the follow zoom,
          in the Flight scene instrument: the charge at 0.45 s, the release at
          0.68 s, steady thrust at 1.1 s and a later release at speed at 5.9 s.
          The ship, fade and bloom are the game’s; the ball is the stamps above.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
  </details>
  <section>
    <h2 id="luminous">02.6 / The same model, a luminous stamp</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Direction carried forward, 11 November 2026.</strong>
      The luminous stamp is close to what plasma should look like. It is the
      direction for plasma’s mark; its details are not yet settled.
    </p>
    <p>
      This example keeps the model of 02.5 and changes only what is stamped.
      Each cached frame is smooth light, built as ion’s stamp is: gradients
      drawn once into the cache, a white-hot core, an amber body and a
      transparent edge. Five lobes drift round the centre between frames, so the
      ball is lumpy and restless without a hard pixel. Each tick a frame is
      stamped behind the nozzle, holding its frame and angle for three ticks so
      the lumps show, a little longer along the exhaust than across it, with two
      of the game’s own small sparks flung from it. The charge is the bead of
      02.1 made smooth: it tightens and whitens live at the nozzle and leaves a
      faint trace in the persistent light.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scene keeps the 0.6 second charge it was made with. It opens just
      after the first release. Drag Time back to 0.3 s for the charge and on to
      1 s for steady thrust; 5 s and 11 s show later releases at speed. In the
      menu, the Luminous ball switch shows the game’s light on the same flight.
    </p>
  </section>
  <InstrumentBand><PlasmaLight026 client:visible /></InstrumentBand>
  <section>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The ball’s size follows delivered thrust, as every plume in the game
        does, so it shrinks as the ship nears its cruise ceiling.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sizes, colours, the number of lobes and frames and the flare’s duration
        are working values.
      </li>
      <li>
        The scene paints once a tick on a fixed burn. It shows how the light
        looks, not how pressing feels.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reference conditions: a Wasp with a Plasma main engine and ion
        stabilizers, at rest at the run start, burn B1, the Follow view, no
        randomness.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="charge-feel">02.7 / How long the charge is, and how it shows</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Accepted, 11 November 2026.</strong>
      Plasma charges for 0.2 seconds, and its charge shows at four times the
      indicator’s size and twice its intensity. With the luminous ball of 02.6
      these are plasma’s values from here on. Plasma stays a preview part: its
      thrust, mass, speed contribution and energy draw have not been decided, so
      this study does not sanction it for scored play. The scene below opens on
      these values and stays adjustable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The luminous ball is close; the hunch is that what remains is the charge:
      how long it lasts and how strongly it shows. This example makes both
      adjustable on the same flight. The stamp, the sparks and the release are
      those of 02.6.
    </p>
    <p>
      The delay was held fixed in 01 at 0.6 seconds. From here it is open. What
      matters is how the charge and release feel, not exact manoeuvrability, so
      a delay anywhere from about 0.1 to 0.9 seconds is acceptable if it feels
      right.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Charge delay.</strong>
        How long plasma charges before it thrusts, from 0.05 to 1.2 seconds.
        This changes the flight itself: the ship leaves later or sooner.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Indicator size.</strong>
        The scale of the bead that shows the charge at the nozzle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Indicator intensity.</strong>
        The strength of the bead’s light, both live on the ship and as its faint
        trace in the persistent light. At zero the charge is dark, as it is in
        the game today.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The three sliders are in the menu in the top-right corner, with the
      current values written under them. The scene opens at the first press;
      press Play to watch the charge and release, and again after a change. The
      burn presses again at 5 s and 11 s.
    </p>
  </section>
  <InstrumentBand><PlasmaLight027 client:visible /></InstrumentBand>
  <section>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The sliders apply to this scene’s flight only; they do not change the
        game.
      </li>
      <li>
        The scene plays a fixed burn, so it shows how a delay looks. How it
        feels under the hand needs the game.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reference conditions: a Wasp with a Plasma main engine and ion
        stabilizers, at rest at the run start, burn B1, the Follow view, no
        randomness.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3 id="accepted">The accepted variant</h3>
    <figure class="plates results">
      <div>
        <img
          src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/accepted-charge.png"
          alt="A Wasp with a wide soft amber glow around its engine and a white point at the nozzle"
          width="300"
          height="300"
        >
        <img
          src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/accepted-release.png"
          alt="A Wasp with lumpy amber fire bursting behind its engine"
          width="300"
          height="300"
        >
        <img
          src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/accepted-thrust.png"
          alt="A Wasp in steady thrust trailing a lumpy amber flame"
          width="300"
          height="300"
        >
        <img
          src="/studies/plasma-light-2026-11-11/accepted-restart.png"
          alt="A Wasp at speed just after a release, with a ball of flame and a fading tail"
          width="300"
          height="300"
        >
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        The accepted variant at four times the follow zoom: the charge at 0.1 s,
        the release at 0.25 s, steady thrust at 0.8 s and a later release at
        speed at 5.4 s. Captured 11 November 2026.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h3>What exactly was accepted</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Charge:</strong>
        0.2 seconds from press to thrust.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Charge light:</strong>
        a smooth bead at the nozzle, white-hot centre through amber to a
        transparent edge, 32 world units in radius at the press and tightening
        to 16 as the charge fills while its centre whitens. It is drawn live on
        LFX at full strength and leaves a trace in FX at 0.4 strength. The
        slider’s 2× is capped at full strength for the live bead, so 2× and 1×
        differ only in the trace.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Thrust light:</strong>
        the luminous ball of 02.6, eight cached frames of five drifting lobes
        round a hot core, 26 units across at full thrust and 1.3 times as long
        as wide, stamped into FX each tick, holding a frame and its angle for
        three ticks, with two small sparks a tick.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Release:</strong>
        the ball starts 2.2 times its size and settles over 0.22 seconds.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Seen on:</strong>
        a Wasp with one Plasma engine, burn B1, no randomness. Other ships and
        several plasma engines together were not looked at.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="readiness">
      03 / The parts exist; the charge has no way into the light
    </h2>
    <p>
      The accepted light can be built from what the game has. The persistent
      surface, cached stamps, the live layer and the plume’s place in the frame
      are all there. One thing is missing: the game’s effects are told how hard
      an engine is thrusting, not that it is charging, so a charging engine
      cannot yet be drawn.
    </p>
    <h3>Reusable as it stands</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The persistent FX surface with its fade, bloom and budget, and the way
        ion caches a complete luminous stamp once and draws it each frame.
      </li>
      <li>
        The engine plume already takes a painter per outlet, and the frame
        already has a live pass on LFX, used by the ion cannon and point
        defence.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Flight scene instrument and this study’s light, as the reference to
        compare production against.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Needs adapting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Engine state for effects.</strong>
        A plume outlet exists only while output is above zero. The plume needs
        each engine’s charge progress and the time since its release, which the
        simulation holds but passes to no effect.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A plasma style.</strong>
        Plasma, like Fission, falls to the shared amber beam. It needs its own
        style, its own multi-frame stamp cache and a painter that places the
        ball behind the nozzle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Engine light on LFX.</strong>
        The live pass draws weapons only. The charge bead has to be added to it,
        as a cached stamp: the study drew its gradient every frame, which the
        effects contract does not allow in play.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The effects contract.</strong>
        It says effects follow delivered output and not button state. A charge
        is a state of the engine, not of the button, but the contract has to say
        so.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Depends on</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The open blocker on engine light: light is stamped once per drawn frame
        with nothing filling the path between, so a fast ship leaves separate
        stamps. A 26-unit ball hides this better than ion’s thin beam but is
        subject to it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Risks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Balance.</strong>
        A shorter charge makes plasma easier to fly while its thrust, mass,
        speed contribution and energy draw stay as they were. Those are
        undecided, which is why plasma stays a preview part.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Size.</strong>
        The bead starts at twice the Wasp’s length across. On a Scout’s two side
        engines or a Corvette’s five mounts the beads and balls will overlap,
        and nobody has looked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost.</strong>
        One large stamp per plasma engine per tick is within the scene budget on
        paper. It has not been measured on the mixed-effects bench.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Evidence.</strong>
        The look was judged on a fixed burn in the instrument, painting once a
        tick. The feel of a 0.2 second delay under the hand has not been tried
        in the game.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Unresolved</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Whether plasma’s ball should shrink as the ship nears its cruise
        ceiling, as every plume does today.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fission keeps the shared amber beam and still needs its own study.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="scope">04 / Four slices, from a number to the light in play</h2>
    <p>
      <strong
        >No change to the experience principles in <code>docs/design.md</code>
        is proposed.</strong
      >
      Answering a press at once is the existing principle that every control
      answers. The effects specification changes instead, in slice 2.
    </p>
    <h3>1 / Plasma charges for 0.2 seconds — small</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Delivered 11 November 2026</strong>
        as a working change, not yet committed: the part’s charge length, the
        Hangar’s description built from it, the charge test following the part’s
        value, and the parts specification.
      </li>
      <li>
        Change the part’s charge length, with the tests, Lab starters and the
        parts specification that state it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check: a held plasma engine first thrusts after the new charge, and
        release or missing power still resets it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>2 / Effects can see an engine charging — small to medium</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Delivered 11 November 2026</strong>
        as a working change, not yet committed: engine charge and thrust time
        reach the plume without painting charging outlets.
      </li>
      <li>
        The plume receives each engine’s charge progress and time since release,
        and keeps an outlet for a charging engine.
      </li>
      <li>
        The effects specification states that an engine’s charge is something
        the game delivers, so its light may show before thrust.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check: a charging engine has an outlet with rising progress and no
        output; ion plumes are pixel-for-pixel what they were.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>3 / Plasma’s thrust light — medium, after 2</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Delivered 11 November 2026</strong>
        as a working change, not yet committed: the shared plasma thrust plume
        and a mixed ion and plasma performance workload.
      </li>
      <li>
        A plasma plume style with its cached frames and painter, in the shared
        effects, used wherever a plasma engine is mounted.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check for fidelity: in the Flight scene instrument, the game’s own light
        matches this study’s light on the reference flight during thrust. Check
        for cost on the mixed-effects bench.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>4 / Plasma’s charge light — medium, after 2</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The bead as a cached stamp, drawn in the live pass with its trace in the
        persistent surface; the render specification’s LFX page gains engine
        light.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check for fidelity as in slice 3, during the charge. Check that pause
        redraws the bead without adding brightness.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Order and limits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Slice 1 can ship alone. Slices 3 and 4 are independent of each other.
      </li>
      <li>
        Provisional: the bead’s and ball’s exact sizes on ships other than the
        Wasp, and whether the ball shrinks near the cruise ceiling.
      </li>
      <li>
        Outside this scope: moving plasma into scored play. That waits for a
        decision on its thrust, mass, speed contribution and energy draw.
      </li>
      <li>This scope sizes the work. It does not start it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

